urasse","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE2MjM4NTU0","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/content-revision-history/commit/8b94aac85714a495f33538d69df123496d355dce","node_id":"C_kwDOUKfvOdoAKDhiOTRhYWM4NTcxNGE0OTVmMzM1MzhkNjlkZjEyMzQ5NmQzNTVkY2U","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/content-revision-history/commit/63a0cbf483c83b078411ad5791d6d6454ed26313","sha":"63a0cbf483c83b078411ad5791d6d6454ed26313","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/content-revision-history/commits/63a0cbf483c83b078411ad5791d6d6454ed26313"}],"sha":"8b94aac85714a495f33538d69df123496d355dce","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/content-revision-history/commits/8b94aac85714a495f33538d69df123496d355dce"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/16238554?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/fbourasse","id":16238554,"login":"fbourasse","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE2MjM4NTU0","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/content-revision-history/commits/63a0cbf483c83b078411ad5791d6d6454ed26313/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-06T11:05:31Z","email":"fbourasse@jahia.com","name":"Florent BOURASSÉ"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-06T11:05:31Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix: GHSA-q67w-prc3-ch5h — four issues from the 1.4.10 blind review (#52)\n\n* fix: re-read chosen revision entries as the viewer, not by list containment\n\nGHSA-q67w-prc3-ch5h #4. RevisionDiffService.compare gated only the history\nnode against the caller''s session, then read both entries via a system\nsession; the only per-entry test was membership of the history''s entry list,\nwhich is built on that same system session. An entry whose ACL is tighter than\nits history (an editor keeping one revision private on an otherwise public\nchangelog) therefore had its label and date returned to a caller the repository\nwould have refused.\n\nresolve() now re-reads each chosen entry through the caller''s own session, in\nthe workspace being rendered, before returning anything about it -- the same\nindistinguishable REASON_NOT_FOUND as a bad identifier. The system session is\nkept for building the entry list and reading snapshots. New viewerMayReadEntry\nseam mirrors viewerMayReadHistory so the fail-closed verdict is unit-asserted\ndirectly.\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>\n\n* fix: pin the capture guard to this node''s own connector, not any loopback\n\nGHSA-q67w-prc3-ch5h #1. The loopback guard matched the host with a regex whose\n(:\\d+)? accepted any port and (/.*)? any trailing text, so a site administrator\ncould set capture.baseUrl to http://127.0.0.1:31337 (collecting the operator''s\ncapture credential at a listener they chose) or end it with ''#'' or ''/?x='' to\nreshape the request line built by buildUrl (storing the server root as the\npage''s public history and reporting success).\n\nreachesJahiaDirectly now delegates to a new addressesEndpoint(baseUrl, expected)\nthat compares against DetectedEndpoint.VALUE in full -- loopback host class,\nexact port, exact context path -- and refuses any query, fragment or userinfo.\nThe runtime credential guard uses an injectable nodeEndpoint (defaulting to the\ndetected connector) so it pins the port at the wire too, not just at save time.\n\naddressesEndpoint takes the expected endpoint as an argument precisely so tests\ncan pin the rule against a fixed connector; the two tests that previously\nasserted the loose behaviour (any port, any path) as correct are rewritten, and\na wire-level test proves the credential is withheld from a wrong loopback port.\nThe server-admin karaf/etc escape hatch is unchanged (accept + warn).\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>\n\n* fix: protect the snapshot record with an ACL, not just a hidden flag\n\nGHSA-q67w-prc3-ch5h #2. Every crh:revisionSnapshot property is ''hidden'', which\nsuppresses it from the edit form but does not refuse a write -- only ''protected''\ndoes, and a protected property cannot be written even by the system session that\ncaptures it. Since 1.4.x restored ACL inheritance on the history root, a\ncontributor with write under /sites/<site>/contents could rewrite what a public\nrevision claims the page said.\n\nrestoreInheritance becomes enforceCuratorReadOnly: it breaks inheritance on the\nhistory root and grants g:privileged the built-in ''reader'' role and nothing\nwritable. Curators keep the read the picker and preview need (both go through\nthe caller''s own session); no contributor can write; the capture''s own system\nsession still writes via ACL bypass. This is 1.3.x''s read-grant done right --\n1.3.x broke inheritance and granted nothing, denying every non-root account --\nwithout 1.4.x''s write leak. Now enforced on both the adopt and create branches,\nso a freshly created root is locked from its first capture.\n\nThe principal g:privileged must be confirmed on a live instance; getting it\nwrong would reintroduce the 1.3.x ''nobody can curate'' failure, so the Cypress\nsuite and a manual check cover what a unit test cannot.\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>\n\n* fix: add a per-site opt-out list for .markdown-published properties\n\nGHSA-q67w-prc3-ch5h #3. The generic markdown fallback emits every text-bearing\nstring property (no allow-list, by design, so nothing is silently lost), and the\nsame generated markdown is served at an opted-in page''s anonymous .markdown URL\n-- so a string property a site keeps out of its templates still reached an\nanonymous visitor.\n\ncapture.excludedProperties names properties, comma- or whitespace-separated, to\ndrop from both the archive and the response. It is a file-only advanced setting\nkept OUT of MANAGED_KEYS, so like the capture credential it is preserved verbatim\nacross panel saves and needs no GraphQL or panel surface. textProperties resolves\nthe node''s site once and skips excluded names; an unresolvable site excludes\nnothing, the safe direction (the default already publishes everything and keeps\nthe record complete).\n\nThe mixin''s opt-in now carries a description stating that opting a page in\npublishes its text content at .markdown, per the advisory''s ask to state the\neffect where the opt-in happens; the README documents the setting and that it\nchanges the archive too, and the credential security note is tightened for #1.\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>\n\n* docs: changelog for GHSA-q67w-prc3-ch5h security fixes\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>\n\n* test: e2e confirmation that the snapshot record is tamper-protected\n\nGHSA-q67w-prc3-ch5h #2. The unit test asserts enforceCuratorReadOnly calls\nsetAclInheritanceBreak(true) and grants ''reader'' to ''g:privileged'' -- two calls\non a mock, not what a real repository then permits. The principal is a guess\nuntil a running instance confirms it, and it fails silently in both directions:\ntoo narrow and no curator can read a snapshot (the 1.3.x failure), too broad or\nunapplied and a contributor can still rewrite the public record.\n\nThis spec confirms it structurally (the reader/privileged GRANT exists on the\nhistory root, and no write-conferring role reaches it even inherited) and\nbehaviourally, driven as a real non-root back-office user: the write is refused\nand the stored hash is unchanged, while the same user can still READ the\nsnapshot. The write test carries a control write to ordinary site content --\nwithout it a refusal passes just as well for a user with no rights anywhere,\nwhich is the easiest way for the file to go green while testing nothing.\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>\n\n* fix: grant curators the privileged role, preserving the real reader set\n\nGHSA-q67w-prc3-ch5h #2, corrected twice by the e2e suite. The first version\ngranted the ''reader'' role to the ''g:privileged'' GROUP. Both halves were wrong,\nand both failed silently with a green unit test, because that test asserts which\nmethods are called on a mock rather than what a repository then permits.\n\n1. There is no curator GROUP. Measured on digitall: the effective ACL of\n   /sites/<site>/contents grants roles mostly to INDIVIDUAL USERS\n   (u:mathias=editor, u:jane=contributor, u:irina=reviewer), and the global\n   g:privileged group carries a DENY, so the grant reached nobody.\n   enforceCuratorReadOnly now copies the principals holding a GRANT on the\n   PARENT -- skipping any carrying a DENY, so a principal being kept out is not\n   let in -- and reads from the parent so a root a previous version already\n   broke is repaired rather than frozen.\n\n2. Roles are WORKSPACE-scoped. Jahia''s ''reader'' role is jcr:read_live alone,\n   and this tree is jmix:nolive: it exists only in default and never reaches\n   live, so granting reader grants read of a workspace the record is not in.\n   Even with a perfect-looking ACL (GRANT reader USER:mathias, inheritance\n   broken) the editor still got PathNotFoundException and jContent showed an\n   empty Content section. The role granted is now ''privileged''\n   (jcr:read_default, no write, no publish); ''reviewer'' also reads default but\n   drags publish and workflow along.\n\n07-jcontentUi caught both -- it drives a real editor through the browser -- and\n13-snapshotRecordTamperProtection now proves the property directly: the write is\nrefused with AccessDeniedException and the stored hash is unchanged, while the\nsame editor still reads the snapshot. Its behavioural assertions go through\ncy.request with basic auth, not cy.apollo: the harness''s apollo client\nauthenticates as its own administrator and ignores cy.login, so those tests were\nrunning as root -- which bypasses the access manager -- and the read assertion\nwas passing for the wrong reason.\n\nFull suite: 110/110 Cypress specs green, 383 unit tests green.\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>\n\n* docs: correct the #2 changelog entry for the preserved-reader ACL fix\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>\n\n---------\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>","tree":{"sha":"d7170e689f9835b51f2716bf1fd8c9807a04eb06","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/content-revision-history/git/trees/d7170e689f9835b51f2716bf1fd8c9807a04eb06"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/content-revision-history/git/commits/63a0cbf483c83b078411ad5791d6d6454ed26313","verification":{"payload":"tree d7170e689f9835b51f2716bf1fd8c9807a04eb06\nparent 4a548109fb81e3d8bae220f7b302c217a89b8160\nauthor Florent BOURASSÉ <fbourasse@jahia.com> 1788692731 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788692731 +0200\n\nfix: GHSA-q67w-prc3-ch5h — four issues from the 1.4.10 blind review (#52)\n\n* fix: re-read chosen revision entries as the viewer, not by list containment\n\nGHSA-q67w-prc3-ch5h #4. RevisionDiffService.compare gated only the history\nnode against the caller''s session, then read both entries via a system\nsession; the only per-entry test was membership of the history''s entry list,\nwhich is built on that same system session. An entry whose ACL is tighter than\nits history (an editor keeping one revision private on an otherwise public\nchangelog) therefore had its label and date returned to a caller the repository\nwould have refused.\n\nresolve() now re-reads each chosen entry through the caller''s own session, in\nthe workspace being rendered, before returning anything about it -- the same\nindistinguishable REASON_NOT_FOUND as a bad identifier. The system session is\nkept for building the entry list and reading snapshots. New viewerMayReadEntry\nseam mirrors viewerMayReadHistory so the fail-closed verdict is unit-asserted\ndirectly.\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>\n\n* fix: pin the capture guard to this node''s own connector, not any loopback\n\nGHSA-q67w-prc3-ch5h #1. The loopback guard matched the host with a regex whose\n(:\\d+)? accepted any port and (/.*)? any trailing text, so a site administrator\ncould set capture.baseUrl to http://127.0.0.1:31337 (collecting the operator''s\ncapture credential at a listener they chose) or end it with ''#'' or ''/?x='' to\nreshape the request line built by buildUrl (storing the server root as the\npage''s public history and reporting success).\n\nreachesJahiaDirectly now delegates to a new addressesEndpoint(baseUrl, expected)\nthat compares against DetectedEndpoint.VALUE in full -- loopback host class,\nexact port, exact context path -- and refuses any query, fragment or userinfo.\nThe runtime credential guard uses an injectable nodeEndpoint (defaulting to the\ndetected connector) so it pins the port at the wire too, not just at save time.\n\naddressesEndpoint takes the expected endpoint as an argument precisely so tests\ncan pin the rule against a fixed connector; the two tests that previously\nasserted the loose behaviour (any port, any path) as correct are rewritten, and\na wire-level test proves the credential is withheld from a wrong loopback port.\nThe server-admin karaf/etc escape hatch is unchanged (accept + warn).\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>\n\n* fix: protect the snapshot record with an ACL, not just a hidden flag\n\nGHSA-q67w-prc3-ch5h #2. Every crh:revisionSnapshot property is ''hidden'', which\nsuppresses it from the edit form but does not refuse a write -- only ''protected''\ndoes, and a protected property cannot be written even by the system session that\ncaptures it. Since 1.4.x restored ACL inheritance on the history root, a\ncontributor with write under /sites/<site>/contents could rewrite what a public\nrevision claims the page said.\n\nrestoreInheritance becomes enforceCuratorReadOnly: it breaks inheritance on the\nhistory root and grants g:privileged the built-in ''reader'' role and nothing\nwritable. Curators keep the read the picker and preview need (both go through\nthe caller''s own session); no contributor can write; the capture''s own system\nsession still writes via ACL bypass. This is 1.3.x''s read-grant done right --\n1.3.x broke inheritance and granted nothing, denying every non-root account --\nwithout 1.4.x''s write leak. Now enforced on both the adopt and create branches,\nso a freshly created root is locked from its first capture.\n\nThe principal g:privileged must be confirmed on a live instance; getting it\nwrong would reintroduce the 1.3.x ''nobody can curate'' failure, so the Cypress\nsuite and a manual check cover what a unit test cannot.\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>\n\n* fix: add a per-site opt-out list for .markdown-published properties\n\nGHSA-q67w-prc3-ch5h #3. The generic markdown fallback emits every text-bearing\nstring property (no allow-list, by design, so nothing is silently lost), and the\nsame generated markdown is served at an opted-in page''s anonymous .markdown URL\n-- so a string property a site keeps out of its templates still reached an\nanonymous visitor.\n\ncapture.excludedProperties names properties, comma- or whitespace-separated, to\ndrop from both the archive and the response. It is a file-only advanced setting\nkept OUT of MANAGED_KEYS, so like the capture credential it is preserved verbatim\nacross panel saves and needs no GraphQL or panel surface. textProperties resolves\nthe node''s site once and skips excluded names; an unresolvable site excludes\nnothing, the safe direction (the default already publishes everything and keeps\nthe record complete).\n\nThe mixin''s opt-in now carries a description stating that opting a page in\npublishes its text content at .markdown, per the advisory''s ask to state the\neffect where the opt-in happens; the README documents the setting and that it\nchanges the archive too, and the credential security note is tightened for #1.\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>\n\n* docs: changelog for GHSA-q67w-prc3-ch5h security fixes\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>\n\n* test: e2e confirmation that the snapshot record is tamper-protected\n\nGHSA-q67w-prc3-ch5h #2. The unit test asserts enforceCuratorReadOnly calls\nsetAclInheritanceBreak(true) and grants ''reader'' to ''g:privileged'' -- two calls\non a mock, not what a real repository then permits. The principal is a guess\nuntil a running instance confirms it, and it fails silently in both directions:\ntoo narrow and no curator can read a snapshot (the 1.3.x failure), too broad or\nunapplied and a contributor can still rewrite the public record.\n\nThis spec confirms it structurally (the reader/privileged GRANT exists on the\nhistory root, and no write-conferring role reaches it even inherited) and\nbehaviourally, driven as a real non-root back-office user: the write is refused\nand the stored hash is unchanged, while the same user can still READ the\nsnapshot. The write test carries a control write to ordinary site content --\nwithout it a refusal passes just as well for a user with no rights anywhere,\nwhich is the easiest way for the file to go green while testing nothing.\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>\n\n* fix: grant curators the privileged role, preserving the real reader set\n\nGHSA-q67w-prc3-ch5h #2, corrected twice by the e2e suite. The first version\ngranted the ''reader'' role to the ''g:privileged'' GROUP. Both halves were wrong,\nand both failed silently with a green unit test, because that test asserts which\nmethods are called on a mock rather than what a repository then permits.\n\n1. There is no curator GROUP. Measured on digitall: the effective ACL of\n   /sites/<site>/contents grants roles mostly to INDIVIDUAL USERS\n   (u:mathias=editor, u:jane=contributor, u:irina=reviewer), and the global\n   g:privileged group carries a DENY, so the grant reached nobody.\n   enforceCuratorReadOnly now copies the principals holding a GRANT on the\n   PARENT -- skipping any carrying a DENY, so a principal being kept out is not\n   let in -- and reads from the parent so a root a previous version already\n   broke is repaired rather than frozen.\n\n2. Roles are WORKSPACE-scoped. Jahia''s ''reader'' role is jcr:read_live alone,\n   and this tree is jmix:nolive: it exists only in default and never reaches\n   live, so granting reader grants read of a workspace the record is not in.\n   Even with a perfect-looking ACL (GRANT reader USER:mathias, inheritance\n   broken) the editor still got PathNotFoundException and jContent showed an\n   empty Content section. The role granted is now ''privileged''\n   (jcr:read_default, no write, no publish); ''reviewer'' also reads default but\n   drags publish and workflow along.\n\n07-jcontentUi caught both -- it drives a real editor through the browser -- and\n13-snapshotRecordTamperProtection now proves the property directly: the write is\nrefused with AccessDeniedException and the stored hash is unchanged, while the\nsame editor still reads the snapshot. Its behavioural assertions go through\ncy.request with basic auth, not cy.apollo: the harness''s apollo client\nauthenticates as its own administrator and ignores cy.login, so those tests were\nrunning as root -- which bypasses the access manager -- and the read assertion\nwas passing for the wrong reason.\n\nFull suite: 110/110 Cypress specs green, 383 unit tests green.\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>\n\n* docs: correct the #2 changelog entry for the preserved-reader ACL fix\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>\n\n---------\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqnUj7CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAiDgQAInV5jyTAdUNxJ6jg+SGVpn4\nfUvFCMv28mXtotUZNOY5IqI/hwlWGMdRjDh2j3tFTAICu9T2B3jsURnLL+uyVPIx\nmf2k5LnPWHFVL1BALZTN7um7pDq+/Lbfc7/pOta/HQ/zYYL/4/0I155mqmkBMp5S\n10QRpmcdgt9wtGWL+dTMLdGXGfK/fsZqSAbf8V4mDdha706FZSairbAB0MF9c3xX\nbr4qJOdOikx0CVaxNjMqtSlUOnIZJWY/Y4GUUuSLJcnEP6QZfiCbDv4LPgTGaUY6\nCckKV8+Pv5zjNYAlXolFvpgYto9lg+Nwt1lMPCgKoy6FqtmSRTzHWRj9Tv4aaaLP\nYE45wI9LYCDDXjJ1R1er0yaz04yj3+RlrWGnsrveq4jIonud9CnQPknI/cnFiPTW\ntZhSxLJVnKq0YveJOkgspikFfY0rtsTRAUbdT9cQZOzOYllA+Oq/E60xrJril9dH\nx2d4HqjGM6ojTiiEl4DtOqJLEUDUDd1EekAFgUDTwWM26ZXh2gqCuc6KgkBjeSg/\n5CUIgy3QRavr9jzdLlLIaOMCJvL5w4o+2C03i1/CNH6BeChZ1RIVYJTVC0e/KKq1\nrKLQr+7XJWtweB7MXdCMVCMWzgVavLWHMI3EQ0DZuIzLr89jWXsS+bUI/HmV3KlK\nix7jwlUYQF/XKlNZMc+r\n=XBMv\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-06T11:05:32Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api